  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. The main ones are `timestamp` (date), `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (boolean), `FlightDelay`, `DestCountry`, `AvgTicketPrice` and `DistanceKilometers`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-63401e9c Flights' (id `2eed36ec-0a48-4b7c-83e0-e64a5a4b1e16`) has two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count on a date histogram of `timestamp`.\n   - \"Flights per carrier\": a bar chart of count split by terms on `Carrier` (top 10). I gave it this placeholder title so step 4 had a rename to make.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-63401e9c Cancelled flights' (id `099161b5-3174-4bec-8c57-640b432616f7`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It uses a KQL filter `Cancelled : true` on the flights data view, a 1-minute check interval, a 5-minute count window, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created enabled, so I did not need to enable it. My first create attempt failed validation (`termSize` must be \u2265 1 when not grouping), so I removed that field and recreated the rule. The failed attempt created nothing.\n4. **Panel rename:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I read the dashboard back and it has two panels, with that title on the second one.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change: the \"over time\" chart is a line chart, the carrier chart is a bar chart, and \"cancelled\" means `Cancelled` is true.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
